Poisson Process
\layout Standard

The Poisson process gives probabilities of a number of arrivals, 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

, as a function of time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, given a `rate', 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P(k|t,\lambda)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

It is a counting process.
 First published by Sim\i \'{e}
on Denis Poisson in 1837.
 
\layout Subsection

Properties
\layout Subsubsection

Mean and Variance
\layout Standard

The 
\emph on 
mean
\emph default 
 and 
\emph on 
variance
\emph default 
 of the process.
 
\layout Standard

The mean of the Poisson process.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle k\right\rangle _{P(k|t,\lambda)}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}k\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}\]

\end_inset 

 Pull out 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda te^{-\lambda t}$
\end_inset 

 and cancel 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{k}{k(k-1)!}$
\end_inset 

, remove first term (
\begin_inset Formula $k=0$
\end_inset 

) which is zero.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
=e^{-\lambda t}\lambda t\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{(\lambda t)^{(k-1)}}{(k-1)!}\]

\end_inset 

 substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $m=k-1$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
=e^{-\lambda t}\lambda t\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(\lambda t)^{m}}{m!}\]

\end_inset 

 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(\lambda t)^{m}}{m!}=e^{\lambda t}$
\end_inset 

 we may write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
=\lambda t\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The variance of a Poisson is also 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda t$
\end_inset 

 --- see Tutorial 1.
 
