\layout Subsubsection

Interarrival Time Distribution
\layout Standard


\begin_inset ERT
status Collapsed

\layout Standard

\backslash 
centering 
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Float figure
wide false
collapsed false

\layout Standard
\align center 

\begin_inset Graphics
	filename ./diagrams/interArrivals.eps

\end_inset 


\layout Caption

Given a set of Poisson arrivals at times 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}\dots T_{n}$
\end_inset 

, the interarrival times are defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}=T_{i}-T_{i-1},i=1\dots n$
\end_inset 

.
 What can we say about the distribution of these times (which are also random
 variables)?
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The distribution dictating these arrival times will be a p.d.f.
 as these times are continuous variables.
 
\layout Standard

The following events are equivalent: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
(X_{i}>t)\equiv(T_{i}-T_{i-1}>t)\equiv(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t)\]

\end_inset 

 The event 
\begin_inset Formula $(T_{i}>T_{i-1}+t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1})$
\end_inset 

 occurs if and only if the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

 does not change during the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $(t_{i-1},t_{i-1}+t)$
\end_inset 

.
 So the following events are equivalent 
\begin_inset Formula \[
X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}\equiv N(t_{i-1}+t)-N(t_{i-1})=0\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
P(X_{i}>t|T_{i-1}=t_{i-1}) & = & P(N(t_{i-1}+t)-N(t_{i-1}=0))\nonumber \\
 & = & P(N(t)=0))\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

From Poisson process 
\begin_inset Formula $P(N(t)=0)=e^{-\lambda t}$
\end_inset 

.
 Which means that the c.d.f.
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $P(X_{i}\leq t)=1-e^{-\lambda t}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

To obtain the pdf we differentiate giving 
\begin_inset Formula $p(X_{i})=\lambda e^{-\lambda X_{i}}$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Section

Mean and Variance of the Exponential Distribution
\layout Standard

Recall the exponential distribution 
\begin_inset Formula \[
p(x)=\lambda e^{-\lambda x}\]

\end_inset 

 The mean of an exponential can be found to be, 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}=\frac{1}{\lambda}\]

\end_inset 

 and the variance is 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\left\langle x^{2}\right\rangle _{p(x)}-\left\langle x\right\rangle _{p(x)}^{2}=\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}.\]

\end_inset 

 These are easily confirmed --- try doing them in your free time! 
\layout Chapter

Discrete Markov Processes
\layout Standard

Perhaps mention discrete time vs discrete state??
\layout Standard

Consider a discrete representation of time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=0,1,2...$
\end_inset 

.
 A state variable 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset 

 changes with time, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(0),N(1),N(2)...$
\end_inset 

.
 If this state variable exhibits the Markov property its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is dependent only on its value at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t-1$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

Monopoly Board 
\layout Standard

There are forty squares.
 
\layout Standard

Let us take the state, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

 to be the number of the square you are on 
\begin_inset Formula $+40$
\end_inset 

 for every time you have reached GO.
 For example if 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)=80$
\end_inset 

 You have circled the board twice and are on GO or if 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)=45$
\end_inset 

 you have been around once and are at King's Cross.
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

 is now associated with the number of player `turns'.
 
\layout Standard

In Monopoly you move your counter through rolling two six sided dice.
 Your state after turn 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t)$
\end_inset 

 depends only on your die role, 
\begin_inset Formula $d(t)$
\end_inset 

, and your state before turn 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula $N(t-1)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
N(t)=N(t-1)+d(t)\]

\end_inset 

 Thus monopoly exhibits the Markov property.
 
