\layout Subsection

Poisson Process as a Markov Process
\layout Standard

Currently, with each turn, we consider time, 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset 

, to change by 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t=1$
\end_inset 

 with each turn.
 Consider a situation where 
\begin_inset Formula $P(h)$
\end_inset 

 is taken to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\,\,\Delta t$
\end_inset 

 (which must be less than 1), giving 
\begin_inset Formula $P(t)=1-\lambda\,\,\Delta t$
\end_inset 

.
 
\layout Standard

Now after 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 turns, what is the probability that the state 
\begin_inset Formula $N=k$
\end_inset 

.
 This is given by a binomial distribution: 
\layout Subsubsection

Binomial for 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 trials
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset 

 = number of trials (turns in our case) with a constant probability of success,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P(h)$
\end_inset 

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset 

 = number of successes (number of heads in our case).
 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \[
P(k)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
n\\
k\end{array}\right)P(h)^{k}(1-P(h))^{n-k}\]

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Here 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\begin{array}{c}
n\\
k\end{array}\right)=\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}$
\end_inset 

 is the binomial coefficient.
 
\layout Standard

Now 
\begin_inset Formula $n=\frac{t}{\Delta t}$
\end_inset 

 which means that 
\begin_inset Formula $P(h)=\lambda\Delta t=\frac{\lambda t}{n}$
\end_inset 

 giving 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray}
P(k|\lambda,t) & = & \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\left(\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{k}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{n-k}\\
 & = & \frac{n\left(n-1\right)\dots\left(n-\left(k-1\right)\right)}{n^{k}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{k}}\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}\left(1-\frac{\lambda t}{n}\right)^{n}\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset 

 If we now take the limit as 
\begin_inset Formula $n\rightarrow\infty$
\end_inset 

 (equivalent to 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t\rightarrow0$
\end_inset 

), this expression becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P(k|\lambda,t)=\frac{(\lambda t)^{k}}{k!}e^{-\lambda t}.\label{eqn:poisson}\end{equation}

\end_inset 

This is because the first factor is equal to 1 as in this limit it becomes
 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\frac{n^{k}}{n^{k}}\]

\end_inset 

and the definition of an exponential function is
\begin_inset Formula \[
\exp\left(z\right)=\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\left(1+\frac{z}{m}\right)^{m}.\]

\end_inset 

 Eqn 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{eqn:poisson}

\end_inset 

 is recognised as a Poisson process.
 In other words, we have proved that a Poisson process is a particular type
 of Markov process.
 
\layout Standard

Coin Monopoly played at infinite speed is a Poisson process! 
